The visual representation of symbols and feedback must be original to DuoCode.

The implementation must not reproduce another manufacturer’s distinctive board layout, peg artwork, branding or visual presentation.

3.2 Secret codes

Each player independently creates a secret code.

A secret code consists of four positions, each containing one of six available symbols.

Repeated symbols are permitted.

Secret codes must remain private until the game has been completed.

3.3 Guessing

Each player independently attempts to discover the opponent’s secret code.

A guess consists of four symbols.

The server validates the guess and calculates feedback.

The feedback consists of:

Exact match: correct symbol in the correct position.
Misplaced match: correct symbol in an incorrect position.
Feedback must not reveal which individual positions are correct or misplaced.

3.4 Feedback calculation

The feedback algorithm must correctly handle duplicate symbols.

The algorithm must count exact matches first, then determine misplaced matches from the remaining unmatched symbols.

It must never count a symbol more times than it occurs in either code.

The algorithm must be independently tested against a separate reference implementation.

3.5 Attempts

Each player has a maximum of ten guesses.

Every valid submitted guess consumes one attempt.

Invalid requests must not consume attempts.

A player who discovers the opponent’s code has solved their challenge.

A player who uses all ten attempts without solving has exhausted their attempts.

3.6 Game completion

The game remains active until both players have either:

Solved the opponent’s code; or
Exhausted their available attempts.
One player’s completion must not prematurely terminate the other player’s challenge.

3.7 Winner determination

The outcome is determined as follows:

