If both players solve in the same number of attempts, the result is a draw.

If neither player solves, the result is a draw.

The final result must include both players’ outcomes and the number of guesses used.

3.8 Results

Once the game is complete, the server may reveal:

Both secret codes.
Both players’ guesses.
Feedback for each guess.
Number of attempts used.
Whether each player solved their challenge.
Final result.
Game completion time.
Secret codes must never be disclosed before the game reaches a completed state.


4. Functional Requirements

4.1 Game management

FR-001 — Create game

The system shall allow a player to create a new game.

FR-002 — Unique game identifier

Every game shall have a unique, non-guessable identifier.

FR-003 — Invitation link

The system shall generate a secure invitation mechanism that can be shared with another player.

FR-004 — Join game

A second player shall be able to join using a valid invitation.

FR-005 — Player identity

Players shall be able to choose a display nickname without creating a permanent account.

FR-006 — Secret code submission

Each player shall be able to submit their secret code.

FR-007 — Game activation

The game shall become active when both players have joined and submitted valid secret codes.

FR-008 — Submit guess

A player shall be able to submit a guess against the opponent’s secret code.

FR-009 — Feedback

The server shall calculate and return the appropriate feedback.

FR-010 — Game status

Players shall be able to retrieve the current game status and their own progress.

FR-011 — Results

The system shall calculate and publish the final result when both players have completed their challenges.

FR-012 — Rematch

The system shall support creating a new game based on a completed game.

FR-013 — Game history

The system shall retain the information required to reconstruct the game and its result.

FR-014 — Game expiry

The system shall support configurable expiry of abandoned games.

4.2 Player management

FR-015 — Independent players

Each player shall have a separate identity within a game.

FR-016 — Player authorisation

Every player action shall be associated with the correct player identity.

FR-017 — Player isolation

One player shall not be able to impersonate the other player.

FR-018 — No mandatory accounts

The initial release shall not require permanent user accounts.

4.3 Game integrity

FR-019 — Server authority

The server shall be authoritative for all game rules, feedback and state transitions.

FR-020 — Attempt validation

The server shall enforce the maximum number of guesses.

FR-021 — Invalid guesses

Invalid guesses shall be rejected without modifying game state.

FR-022 — Completed games

Completed games shall not accept further guesses.

FR-023 — Duplicate request protection

The API shall protect against accidental duplicate submissions caused by client retries.

FR-024 — Concurrent actions

Simultaneous requests shall not cause inconsistent game state or incorrect attempt counts.


5. Non-functional Requirements

5.1 Performance

Normal API operations should respond within 500 ms at the 95th percentile under the initial expected load.
Game operations should not require long-lived connections.
The system should support multiple concurrent games.
Performance shall be measured rather than assumed.
5.2 Availability

The service should be suitable for continuous operation.
Temporary service interruptions must not corrupt game state.
Database transactions shall protect critical game operations.
Failed requests must not leave partially updated games.
5.3 Security

All external communications shall use HTTPS.
Player access tokens shall be cryptographically secure.
Invitation tokens shall be sufficiently random to prevent guessing.
Tokens shall be stored securely, preferably as hashes where appropriate.
Secret codes shall not appear in unauthorised API responses.
Inputs shall be validated server-side.
Rate limiting shall protect public endpoints.
Administrative access shall be separated from player access.
Security-sensitive information shall not appear in ordinary application logs.
5.4 Privacy

No mandatory personal information shall be collected.
Nicknames shall not require real identities.
Personal information shall not be exposed to opponents.
Data retention and deletion policies shall be documented.
The application shall be designed with applicable UK GDPR requirements in mind.
5.5 Accessibility

Future browser interfaces shall:

Support keyboard navigation.
Use sufficient colour contrast.
Avoid relying exclusively on colour to communicate feedback.
Provide accessible labels for symbols and game actions.
Support screen readers.
Use responsive layouts.
5.6 Maintainability

Game logic shall be independent of HTTP handling.
API contracts shall be documented.
Database migrations shall be version controlled.
Automated tests shall run in CI.
Development and production configuration shall be separated.

