9. Automated Testing and Game Simulation

9.1 Testing strategy

Automated testing is a core development requirement, not an optional final activity.

Testing shall cover:

Unit tests.
Game engine tests.
API integration tests.
Database tests.
Security tests.
Complete game lifecycle tests.
Automated game simulation.
Load and concurrency tests.
9.2 Game engine unit tests

The game engine shall be tested independently of the API.

Tests shall cover:

Exact matches.
Misplaced matches.
No matches.
All positions matching.
Duplicate symbols in the secret code.
Duplicate symbols in guesses.
Mixed duplicate scenarios.
Invalid code lengths.
Invalid symbols.
Attempt limits.
Game completion.
Winner determination.
The feedback algorithm shall be tested against an independent reference implementation.

The reference implementation must not reuse the production feedback calculation.

9.3 API integration tests

Integration tests shall exercise the complete game lifecycle.

Tests shall use separate player credentials.

They shall cover:

Successful game creation.
Successful joining.
Code submission.
Game activation.
Guess submission.
Feedback.
Player progress.
Game completion.
Final results.
Rematches.
9.4 Negative tests

The test suite shall verify that:

A third player cannot join a full game.
A player cannot access another player’s credentials.
A player cannot retrieve the opponent’s secret code prematurely.
A player cannot submit guesses against their own code.
Invalid guesses do not consume attempts.
Attempts cannot exceed the configured limit.
Completed games reject further guesses.
Invalid invitation tokens are rejected.
Expired games cannot be modified.
Repeated requests do not duplicate guesses.
Concurrent submissions cannot corrupt game state.
9.5 Automated game simulator

A dedicated game simulation tool shall be developed.

The simulator shall communicate with the application exclusively through the public REST API.

It shall not bypass API validation or manipulate the database directly.

Simulation requirements

SIM-001 — Support configurable numbers of games.

SIM-002 — Support configurable concurrency.

SIM-003 — Create two independent player identities per game.

SIM-004 — Support deterministic random seeds.

SIM-005 — Implement multiple player strategies.

SIM-006 — Verify feedback against an independent reference implementation.

SIM-007 — Verify game-state transitions.

SIM-008 — Detect unexpected API responses.

SIM-009 — Record failed games for reproduction.

SIM-010 — Produce machine-readable JSON output.

SIM-011 — Produce a human-readable test summary.

SIM-012 — Run as an independent command-line application.

Player strategies

The simulator should initially support:

Random guessing.
Systematic guessing.
Logic-based code-breaking.
The strategies should be independently implemented.

Simulation metrics

The simulator shall report:

Games started.
Games completed.
Wins and draws.
Average guesses.
Maximum guesses.
API errors.
Invalid state transitions.
Response latency.
Concurrency failures.
Unexpected game outcomes.
Deterministic replay

Every simulation shall support a reproducible random seed.

A failed game shall be identifiable by its seed and game number.

The simulator shall permit the same game to be replayed independently.

Example:

deno task simulate \
  --games 1000 \
  --seed 12345 \
  --concurrency 20
The exact command-line interface shall be finalised during implementation.

9.6 Load and concurrency testing

Load tests shall exercise:

Multiple games being created simultaneously.
Multiple players joining simultaneously.
Concurrent guess submissions.
Database transaction handling.
API response times.
Application resource consumption.
The system shall not lose or duplicate valid game operations under supported concurrency.

9.7 Continuous integration

Automated tests shall be integrated into GitHub Actions.

A pull request shall trigger:

Formatting checks.
Static analysis.
Type checking.
Unit tests.
API integration tests.
Relevant security checks.
Build verification.
Simulation and larger load tests may run as separate CI jobs.


