11. White-label and Future Multi-tenant Architecture

11.1 Product strategy

DuoCode shall be designed with future white-label opportunities in mind.

White-label functionality is a product strategy and an architectural constraint, not an MVP feature.

The initial release shall operate as a single-tenant application.

11.2 Architectural separation

The architecture shall maintain separation between:

Core game engine.
Game management and API.
Presentation layer.
Branding and visual configuration.
Tenant configuration.
The core game engine shall not contain presentation-specific assumptions.

11.3 Future white-label capabilities

Future deployments may support:

Custom product names.
Customer logos.
Custom colour schemes.
Custom typography.
Custom domains and subdomains.
Custom welcome screens.
Configurable game rules.
Additional customer-specific functionality.
Independent hosting.
Centrally managed hosting.
11.4 Multi-tenancy readiness

The initial architecture shall not prevent future multi-tenant operation.

Future tenant support shall provide:

Tenant identification.
Tenant-specific configuration.
Logical isolation of tenant data.
Tenant-specific branding.
Tenant-specific game configuration.
Server-side tenant validation.
The initial implementation shall not introduce unnecessary multi-tenant infrastructure.

11.5 White-label commercial model

White-label deployments may be offered under a separate commercial licence.

Potential customers include:

Clubs and societies.
Schools and educational organisations.
Businesses.
Event organisers.
Promotional and marketing organisations.

12. Scalability and Deployment

12.1 Initial deployment

The initial deployment shall use a single Linux VPS.

The deployment shall use Docker Compose.

PostgreSQL shall initially operate as the authoritative database.

12.2 Scalability requirements

SCALE-001 — Stateless application architecture.

SCALE-002 — Horizontal application scaling shall be possible.

SCALE-003 — PostgreSQL shall remain the authoritative persistent store.

SCALE-004 — Redis may be introduced when justified by measured requirements.

SCALE-005 — The architecture shall support reverse proxies and load balancing.

SCALE-006 — Background processing shall be possible without changing the core game engine.

SCALE-007 — Application and database monitoring shall be supported.

SCALE-008 — Deployment shall remain Docker-compatible.

SCALE-009 — Load and concurrency testing shall be available.

SCALE-010 — Avoid unnecessary dependence on a particular cloud provider.

SCALE-011 — The application shall handle temporary infrastructure failures gracefully.

SCALE-012 — Scaling shall be incremental and based on measured demand.

12.3 Real-time communication

The initial implementation shall use ordinary REST requests and polling where required.

WebSockets are not required for the asynchronous game model.

12.4 Infrastructure monitoring

The system should expose metrics for:

Games created.
Active games.
Completed games.
Daily active players where measurable without unnecessary personal tracking.
API requests.
Response latency.
Database utilisation.
Application resource consumption.
Hosting costs.
12.5 Deferred infrastructure

The following are not required for the initial release:

Kubernetes.
Microservices.
Multi-region deployment.
Mandatory Redis.
Distributed event streaming.
Complex service meshes.

13. Licensing and Intellectual Property

13.1 Community licence

The intended public software licence is:

GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 only (AGPL-3.0-only).

The licence shall apply to the community distribution of DuoCode.

The project shall include the complete AGPL 3.0 licence text and appropriate copyright notices.

